+# Case-Insensitive Matching Belongs in the Query, Not the Schema
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-004` narrows the veterinarian directory to one named specialty, matching the name whole and disregarding letter case. The project already matches case-insensitively in one place — owner search — and it does so through the schema: H2 types the column `VARCHAR_IGNORECASE`, MySQL relies on its default collation, PostgreSQL does not fold at all. That third gap is a recorded defect against `REQ-OWN-002`: the same search returns different results per vendor, and the H2-backed default test suite cannot observe it.
+
+The specialty name is worse placed than the owner's last name. `specialties.name` is a plain `VARCHAR`/`TEXT` on all three vendors, so a plain equality query would match case-sensitively under H2 and PostgreSQL and case-insensitively under MySQL — a fresh instance of the same divergence, with the default suite again blind to two thirds of it.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Fold in the schema, per vendor** — retype the H2 column, add a `citext` column or lower-cased functional index under PostgreSQL, lean on the MySQL collation. Three hand-maintained edits reaching the same behavior three ways, in the exact shape that produced the owner-search defect.
+2. **Hand-written JPQL with `lower()`** — one explicit `@Query` on the repository. Vendor-independent, but adds a query string to an interface whose every other method is derived from its name.
+3. **The Spring Data `IgnoreCase` keyword** — the derived-query form; Spring Data emits the case-folded comparison itself.
+
+## Decision
+
+Option 3. Case-insensitive matching is expressed in the query. The database is not asked to carry the rule, and no schema changes for the sake of case folding.
+
+The reason is testability as much as consistency: a rule the query carries behaves identically on every vendor and is provable by the H2-backed suite. A rule the schema carries is realized once per vendor and is only as good as the vendor whose schema the tests happen to run against.
+
+Option 2 stays available where a derived name cannot express the comparison; the keyword is preferred while it can.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Specialty matching is identical under H2, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, and the difference is provable without running the suite against all three.
+- The case-folded comparison does not use the plain index on `specialties.name`. At the seeded scale this costs nothing; a functional index would be the remedy if it ever did.
+- Owner search is **not** fixed by this decision. It keeps its schema-carried rule and its PostgreSQL defect — see [system-design.md](../system-design.md#known-defects). This ADR sets the rule for matching added from here on; retrofitting owner search is a separate change against `REQ-OWN-002`.
+- The project now folds case two ways. The rule states which one new code takes, and the deviation is named rather than left to be discovered.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md: Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence)
+- [ADR: Script-Managed Schema Across Three Database Vendors](2026-07-31-script-managed-multi-vendor-schema.md)
