+# The Veterinarian Cache Is Not Keyed on Caller Input
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Both existing `VetRepository` reads are annotated `@Cacheable("vets")`, and the obvious way to add the specialty-filtered reads `REQ-VET-004` needs is to copy that annotation onto them. Doing so would move the cache key from a closed set to an open one: the unfiltered reads key on nothing and on the page request, whereas a filtered read keys on a string an anonymous caller supplies.
+
+The `vets` cache has no eviction policy and no size limit. `CacheConfiguration` creates it through the JCache API, whose own source comment records that a size limit has to come from the provider's configuration mechanism, and no such configuration exists in the repository. That the cache never evicts is already an open question — asked there about staleness, since nothing writes veterinarians. Keying on caller input turns the same absence into an availability problem: every distinct string a caller invents becomes a resident entry, and repeating the request with fresh strings grows the heap without bound.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the filtered reads like their siblings** — consistent to read, and it hands any unauthenticated caller a memory-growth lever on a system that has no authentication at all.
+2. **Cache them behind a bounded provider configuration** — correct in the long run, but it changes shared cache infrastructure to serve one slice, and the bound would have to be chosen with no traffic to choose it from.
+3. **Leave the filtered reads uncached** — the cache keeps its closed key space; filtered reads reach the database.
+
+## Decision
+
+Option 3. The filtered reads are not cached. `@Cacheable` stays on the two unfiltered reads, whose keys are closed.
+
+The rule this records is the general one: nothing keyed on caller-supplied input enters a cache that has no bound. Bounding the cache first (option 2) would make caching a filtered read legitimate, and that is the route to take when a filtered read is shown to be hot. Neither condition holds now — the seeded directory is six veterinarians, and a database round trip for a filtered page costs less than the reasoning needed to make the cache safe.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The `vets` cache key space stays closed, so no caller can grow it. The application has no authentication, so this is the only control available.
+- Every filtered directory request reaches the database, while the unfiltered directory keeps its cache. `REQ-VET-001`'s behavior and performance are untouched.
+- The two `VetRepository` reads are annotated and their filtered counterparts are not, which reads as an inconsistency without this record. The rule in [system-design.md](../system-design.md#persistence) links here.
+- The cache still has no eviction and no size limit. This decision routes around that gap rather than closing it; the open question stands.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md: Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence)
+- [system-design.md: Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey)
